Title: Question : ValueTask or warning because of not using await
Description: Hi

I have an interface from an external library which i have to adhere by.
It looks like this :
```
public Task<RealestateObject> Handle(RealEstatePushRequestModel request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
}
```
I only have synchronous code that is required in this block.
Is it better make the function async and just return the result : 
```
public async Task<RealestateObject> Handle(RealEstatePushRequestModel request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    //Much synchronous code here...
    return new RealestateObject();
}
```
Which warns me :
**This async method lacks 'await' operators and will run synchronously. Consider using the 'await' operator to await non-blocking API calls, or 'await Task.Run(...)' to do** 

**OR**

I await ValueTask and return that : 
```
public async Task<RealestateObject> Handle(RealEstatePushRequestModel request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    return await GetValue(request);
}

public ValueTask<RealestateObject> GetValue(RealEstatePushRequestModel request)
{
    //Much synchronous code here...
    return new ValueTask<RealestateObject>(new RealestateObject() { ObjectKey = "dummydata" });
}
```
Which has no warnings at all.

**OR** 

use **Task.FromResult**.
```
public Task<RealestateObject> Handle(RealEstatePushRequestModel request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    //Much synchronous code here...
    return Task.FromResult(new RealestateObject() { ObjectKey = "dummydata" });
}
```

Which one of the scenario's is the best to use ?

Thanks!


